{
  "image_name": "73793dc7f3a8eb896849aff4b5ea291d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for advanced hydrogel or resin-based biofabrication. Overall, it shows how printed materials can be varied during fabrication, combined with other manufacturing methods, modified after printing, and designed to respond over time.\n\nOne part illustrates multi-material printing, where different resins are arranged either side-by-side across the x–y plane or stacked along the z direction, creating structures with spatially distinct composition. Another section shows hybrid fabrication approaches that pair light-based printing with methods such as microfluidics, photo ablation, melt electrowriting, or extrusion, suggesting ways to integrate fine internal features or embedded filament networks into printed constructs.\n\nThe figure also highlights post-print functionalization, where already printed gels can be locally patterned with biochemical signals or mechanically tuned to become stiffer or softer in selected regions. A related concept, grayscale projection, uses a gradient of light intensity so that areas receiving more light become stiffer, while lower intensity produces softer hydrogel.\n\nFinally, the bottom panels emphasize added control through multi-wavelength printing and 4D responsiveness. Different wavelengths can trigger separate or cooperative reactions, and printed materials can later shrink, grow, or change shape in response to thermal, pH, or other reactive stimuli.",
